<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10168"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 11:48 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Y&#39;all - I&#39;m a tool. <i>&#40;no need for response to <b>that</b>&#41;.</i>  <BR> <BR>I totally forgot to point out that catchbtk was mentioned too... <BR> <BR>article link - <a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/business/aptech_story.asp?category=1700&amp;slug=BTK%20Internet%20Sleuths">Seattle Post-Intelligencer</a> <BR> <BR>Quote: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...There was someone who signed in as &#39;Dogcatcher,&#39;&#34; said Angela Avey, one of the site administrators for the California-based Web site catchbtk.com. &#34;That&#39;s kind of an odd thing to sign in as. Now we&#39;re kicking ourselves for not finding out more.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Apologies to Tom, Angela, and Ed for my tunnelvision!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
